  "record": {
    "read_status": "partial",
    "file_problem": "Repository metadata, README, and a truncated file listing only; code was not read or run.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The README describes refusal-direction projections and alignment analyses as activation-based baselines for a logit-observability tool. It reports no ablation or addition of the claimed difference-in-means direction.",
    "gate_note": "The README reports early-stop ASR results and an aggregate alignment result for hidden-state refusal directions. These do not test whether ablating or adding the claimed direction changes refusal. The alignment result is observational, and the README says TLO does not identify causal mechanisms.",
    "test_reason": "A logit-observability tool repo. It computes difference-in-means refusal directions only as an activation baseline (B1) and as an observational alignment check for its logit-margin signal. It never ablates, adds or weight-edits the direction, and it states that TLO does not identify causal mechanisms. None of its results bear on whether any version holds.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is essentially correct. TLO is an observational, logit-only diagnostic that separates jailbreak dynamics ASR conflates. It aligns with hidden-state refusal directions in 10/12 conditions (Qwen the boundary), and a derived halt rule cuts ASR from 39.6% to 13.1%. The authors state it does not identify causal mechanisms.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Tool repo, not a replication: it computes temporal compliance–refusal logit margins and evaluates a logit-based halt rule; it does not implement the claimed difference-in-means direction ablation or addition.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The README notes Qwen as a model-specific boundary for the reported hidden-state-direction alignment; no per-condition details are given.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "A t_cross-derived early-stop rule (w=5) cuts aggregate jailbreak ASR from 39.6% to 13.1%. The effect is zero on MCM in all four models and large on DI and on Qwen GCG.",
        "proposed_claim": "Halting on a sign change in the compliance–refusal logit margin reduces jailbreak ASR, depending on attack type.",
